Guard NSViewFrameDidChangeNotification against a view that is not (yet)
installed in a window. pop-os/winit's macOS backend panics in this handler via
`self.window().expect("view must be installed in a window")`, which fires during
the temporary style-mask toggle done by `WindowDelegate::is_zoomed` at startup
(reached from iced's `window::Action::GetMaximized`). Bail out early instead of
panicking when there is no window.

diff --git a/src/view.rs b/src/view.rs
index 6fce2bd..fe9f2af 100644
--- a/src/view.rs
+++ b/src/view.rs
@@ -172,6 +172,13 @@ define_class!(
         #[unsafe(method(viewFrameDidChangeNotification:))]
         fn frame_did_change(&self, _notification: Option<&AnyObject>) {
             trace_scope!("NSViewFrameDidChangeNotification");
+            // This notification can fire while the view is temporarily not
+            // installed in a window (e.g. the style-mask toggle performed by
+            // `WindowDelegate::is_zoomed` at startup). `self.window()` would
+            // panic in that state, so there is nothing to do here.
+            if (**self).window().is_none() {
+                return;
+            }
             if let Some(tracking_rect) = self.ivars().tracking_rect.take() {
                 self.removeTrackingRect(tracking_rect);
             }
